# Line-ending policy.
#
# The developer distribution assets under nextjs/public/developer/ are served
# byte-for-byte from tavonel.com and are pinned by sha256 in channel.json, which
# lib/developer-distribution.test.ts verifies. Without an explicit policy those
# files are checked out through core.autocrlf, so their bytes -- and therefore
# their digests -- differ between a Windows working tree and a Linux CI runner.
# That is exactly how channel.json came to hold digests that matched no committed
# state: they were computed from a CRLF checkout, so the assertion passed locally
# on Windows and failed on ubuntu-latest.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes the working tree, the committed blob, the CI checkout and
# the bytes a customer downloads identical on every platform. Any file whose
# content is hashed, signed, or served verbatim belongs here.
* text=auto

nextjs/public/developer/*.mjs text eol=lf
nextjs/public/developer/*.py text eol=lf
nextjs/public/developer/*.json text eol=lf
nextjs/public/developer/*.md text eol=lf

# SQL migrations are applied by digest-sensitive tooling and read by tests that
# assert on exact text; keep them stable across platforms for the same reason.
supabase/migrations/*.sql text eol=lf
supabase/tests/*.sql text eol=lf

# The /explore sample PDFs are the source documents the public Explore page compiles, and
# lib/explore-sample.test.ts asserts their bytes reproduce the generator's output exactly and
# that each sha256 matches the digest the page displays. Passing them through core.autocrlf
# would rewrite bytes inside a binary stream: the digests would move, the byte-equality test
# would fail on one platform and pass on the other, and a visitor opening the PDF would be
# checking a file the page no longer describes.
nextjs/public/explore-sample/*.pdf binary

# The SEC EDGAR primary documents under sec-source/ are the acquired source bytes of the four
# 2026 filings: sec-corpus-manifest.json pins each one's sha256, and build-explore-sample.mjs
# re-hashes the file on disk and refuses to build when the two disagree. Through core.autocrlf a
# Windows checkout rewrote every line ending, so the acquired original hashed to a value the
# acquisition record had never seen. `-text` is the strongest statement available: these bytes
# are evidence, and git rewrites them in neither direction on any platform.
nextjs/public/explore-sample/sec-source/*.html -text

# The frozen USKC contract schemas are copied verbatim into both repositories and pinned by
# sha256 against the contract artifact. Same rule as above: through core.autocrlf a Windows
# checkout would hash differently from the Linux one, and the two repositories would be
# validating against files that are no longer the same bytes.
shared/*.schema.json text eol=lf

# Imported executable script: keep its hashbang compatible with module transforms.
nextjs/scripts/secret-scan.mjs text eol=lf

# Every script that opens with `#!/usr/bin/env node`, for that reason and one more. Through
# core.autocrlf the hashbang is checked out as `#!/usr/bin/env node\r`, which breaks the file
# twice: Linux resolves the interpreter as `node\r` and reports no such file, and the module
# transform applied to an imported .mjs fails on the stray carriage return with
# `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token` reported against the importing line. That is how
# lib/journey-acceptance.test.ts passed in the lane that wrote it -- where the files had never
# round-tripped through a checkout -- and failed on the first fresh checkout of the same commit.
nextjs/scripts/check-type-floor.mjs text eol=lf
nextjs/scripts/fetch-explore-sec-corpus.mjs text eol=lf
nextjs/scripts/verify-developer-clean-install.mjs text eol=lf
nextjs/scripts/journey/*.mjs text eol=lf
nextjs/scripts/qa/*.mjs text eol=lf

# Every node script under scripts/ opens with a hashbang, and lib/journey-acceptance.test.ts
# fails on a CRLF one: a Linux shell resolves the interpreter as `node`, and the module
# transform applied to an imported .mjs stops on the stray carriage return. The individual pins
# above predate this line and are kept; this one covers the class, including the three scripts a
# fresh Windows checkout was still getting CRLF hashbangs on.
nextjs/scripts/**/*.mjs text eol=lf
nextjs/scripts/encode-cut-2x.sh text eol=lf

# The two reference verifiers are copied verbatim to nextjs/public/developer/ as
# tavonel-verify-export.mjs and tavonel-verify-package.mjs, pinned by sha256 in
# channel.json, and lib/compiler-contract.test.ts asserts the published copy is
# byte-identical to its source. The published copies are already eol=lf above;
# without the same pin here their sources are checked out CRLF on Windows, the
# byte comparison fails on one platform or the other, and whichever digest was
# computed from the wrong checkout is served to customers. Same rule as the rest
# of this file: content that is hashed or served verbatim gets an explicit eol.
nextjs/scripts/verify-signed-export.mjs text eol=lf
nextjs/scripts/compiled-world/validate.mjs text eol=lf
nextjs/scripts/compiled-world/verify-external-roundtrip.py text eol=lf

# Research receipts are evidence artifacts: their published sha256 was computed over the bytes as
# written (CRLF, on the machine that produced them), and a receipt's hash is never rewritten.
# eol=lf would make the served bytes differ from the printed digest on every Linux checkout, which
# is exactly what CI caught on 2026-09-16. So they are stored and served as-is, no conversion.
nextjs/public/research/receipts/*.json -text

# Deterministic browser icon representations
nextjs/public/brand/*.svg text eol=lf
nextjs/public/icon.svg text eol=lf

# Committed benchmark artifacts. Each one is bound by sha256 in a lib module and rehashed by a
# test, so their bytes must be identical in a Windows working tree and on a Linux CI runner.
nextjs/content/benchmarks/*.json text eol=lf

# Operator scripts carry a hashbang and are executed directly by a shell. A CRLF checkout puts the
# carriage return inside the interpreter path; lib/journey-acceptance.test.ts pins it. That is also
# why vitest could not import scripts/db/*.test.mjs at all -- the transform stopped on the carriage
# return in the script the test imports and blamed the import line. The glob covers every script,
# so the deletion drills cannot pick the same defect back up.
nextjs/scripts/**/*.mjs text eol=lf
